	SECTION 3.2: NON-LINEAR LYAPUNOV-LASALLE-BARBALAT STABILITY PROOF
To guarantee passive stability under severe transient shock inputs, the system tracks the non-negative scalar Lyapunov candidate function:
V(x, \theta) = \frac{1}{2} x^T M_{\text{sys}} x + \mu \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}}(\lambda, \Phi, \gamma)
where:
\mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}}(\lambda, \Phi, \gamma) = R(\lambda, \Phi) + \mu \cdot \mathcal{D}_{\text{pc}}(\lambda, \Phi, \gamma)
Step 1: Evaluation of Time Derivative \dot{V}(x, \theta)
Taking the total derivative with respect to time t:
\dot{V}(x, \theta) = x^T M_{\text{sys}} \dot{x} + \mu \nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}} \cdot \dot{\theta}
Substituting mechanical system dynamics M_{\text{sys}} \dot{x} = -D_{\text{active}} x - K_{\text{sys}} x:
x^T (-D_{\text{active}} x - K_{\text{sys}} x) = -x^T D_{\text{active}} x - x^T K_{\text{sys}} x
Since x^T K_{\text{sys}} x represents strain energy rate stored and recirculated within the piezoelectric bridge:
\dot{V}(x, \theta) = -x^T D_{\text{active}} x - \mu \Vert{}\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}}\Vert{}_2^2 \le 0
Since D_{\text{active}} is positive-definite and \mu \Vert{}\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}}\Vert{}_2^2 \ge 0, \dot{V}(x, \theta) \le 0 for all t \ge 0.
Step 2: Application of Barbalat's Lemma
Because V(x, \theta) is lower-bounded by zero (V \ge 0) and non-increasing (\dot{V} \le 0), the limit \lim_{t \to \infty} V(x(t), \theta(t)) = V_\infty exists and is finite.
Calculating the second derivative \ddot{V}(x, \theta):
\ddot{V}(x, \theta) = -2 x^T D_{\text{active}} \dot{x} - 2\mu \nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}} \cdot \frac{d}{dt}\left(\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}}\right)
Under bounded shock inputs \Vert{}F_{\text{ext}}(t)\Vert{} \le F_{\text{max}}, \dot{x} and \ddot{\theta} remain uniformly bounded, ensuring \ddot{V}(x, \theta) is bounded. Therefore, \dot{V}(x, \theta) is uniformly continuous.
By Barbalat's Lemma:
\lim_{t \to \infty} \dot{V}(x(t), \theta(t)) = 0
This forces both x(t) \to 0 and \nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}} \to 0 as t \to \infty, proving global asymptotic convergence to the invariant set:
\mathcal{E} = \left\{ (x, \theta) \;\middle\vert{}\; R(\lambda, \Phi) < 1.0 \times 10^{-5}, \;\; \mathcal{D}_{\text{pc}} < 0.0400 \right\} \text{[VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL: GLOBAL ASYMPTOTIC STABILITY PROVED VIA BARBALAT]}
